de, if it is removable (removable=True), not root, and all its children are not retained (i.e., it has no children that are still in the retained set), then we tentatively check if its removal would still leave all required symbols present in the retained tree. If yes, we remove it (i.e., remove from retained set). The retained set initially includes all nodes. After removal, we need to update the set of symbols present. We can compute the set of symbols from the retained nodes. But we also need to consider that when we remove a node, its children are already removed? Actually if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. So we only remove leaves. So we don't need to worry about children removal; they are already removed or not present. So the algorithm simplifies.\n\nBut wait: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node that is a required symbol? That is \"required_symbols\" list. So we cannot remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols? The required_symbols are symbols that must be present in the retained tree. So we cannot remove a node if its symbol is one of the required symbols, because that would cause that symbol to be missing? However, there could be multiple nodes with the same symbol. The requirement \"all required symbols remain represented\" means there must exist at least one node with that symbol in the retained tree. So if a node's symbol is required but there are other nodes with that symbol, we can remove it. But the rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" which might mean a node whose symbol is in the required list. But that would be too strict. Let's re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" I think \"required symbol\" refers to nodes that are themselves marked as required? But the payload has \"required_symbols\" as a list of symbols. So it's likely that we cannot remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols and it 